---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SIPCalculator from '../../components/calculators/engines/SIPCalculator';
import AffiliateCTA from '../../components/AffiliateCTA.astro';
import { BETTERMENT } from '../../lib/affiliates';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Investment Calculator - Long-Term Growth Projections | PennyMath"
  description="Free investment calculator. Project your portfolio growth with monthly contributions and compound returns. Visualize the long-term power of consistent investing."
  keywords="investment calculator, portfolio calculator, brokerage calculator, monthly investment calculator, long term investment"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "name": "Investment Calculator",
      "url": "https://pennymath.techtools365.com/us/investment-calculator",
      "applicationCategory": "FinanceApplication",
      "operatingSystem": "Any",
      "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" }
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "What's a realistic investment return?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Plan around 7% real annual return for a diversified equity portfolio over 20+ year horizons — that's the long-term S&P 500 average after inflation. Some years will be -30%, others +30%. Be skeptical of any plan assuming 12%+ — that's optimistic in the current environment." }},
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Index funds vs individual stocks?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "For 95% of investors, broad market index funds win. They have lower fees (0.03-0.15% vs typical mutual fund 0.5-1.5%), better diversification, and historically beat 85-90% of actively managed funds over 15+ year periods. VOO (S&P 500), VTI (total US market), and VXUS (international) are the core building blocks for most portfolios." }},
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Should I invest in a lump sum or DCA?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Lump sum wins about 65% of the time historically — markets trend up. But dollar-cost averaging (DCA) reduces the regret risk of buying at a peak. If lump sum gives you anxiety, DCA over 6-12 months. The worst choice is sitting in cash 'waiting for a dip' — opportunity cost compounds." }},
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much should I invest monthly?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "After capturing 401(k) employer match and maxing Roth IRA, invest 15-20% of gross income toward retirement total. For shorter-term goals (5-10 years), figure out what you need to save and work backward — this calculator helps with that math." }}
      ]
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <nav class="breadcrumbs">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span>/</span>
    <a href="/us/">US</a> <span>/</span>
    Investment Calculator
  </nav>

  <h1>Investment Calculator</h1>
  <p class="page-subtitle">Project the future value of your portfolio</p>
  <p class="page-intro">
    See how monthly investments grow over time at any expected return. Useful for taxable brokerage accounts, mutual funds, ETFs, or any periodic investment plan.
  </p>

  <SIPCalculator
    client:visible
    currency={{ code: 'USD', symbol: '$' }}
    defaults={{ monthlyInvestment: 1000, expectedReturn: 7, timePeriod: 20 }}
  />

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>The Three Levers of Investment Growth</h2>
    <p>You have three levers — contribution amount, return rate, and time. Only two are really under your control:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Time</strong> — the most powerful. $500/month for 40 years at 7% = $1.31M. The same $500/month for 30 years = $611k. Ten extra years more than doubles the outcome.</li>
      <li><strong>Contribution</strong> — fully under your control. Doubling monthly contributions doubles the final balance (over the same period and rate).</li>
      <li><strong>Return rate</strong> — partially under your control via asset allocation, but the broad market sets the ceiling. Don't plan around above-market returns.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The implication: aggressive savers in their 20s and 30s have the easiest path. Aggressive savers in their 50s have to compensate by saving more dollars to make up for fewer years.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>Where to Hold Investments</h2>
    <p>Order of operations for long-term investing (after a 3-6 month emergency fund):</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>401(k) up to employer match</strong> — guaranteed instant return</li>
      <li><strong>HSA</strong> (if eligible) — triple tax advantage</li>
      <li><strong>Roth IRA</strong> — $7,000/year of tax-free growth</li>
      <li><strong>401(k) up to $23,500</strong> — full tax deferral</li>
      <li><strong>Taxable brokerage</strong> — uncapped, fully flexible</li>
      <li><strong>529 plan</strong> — for kids' education, state tax breaks vary</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Once tax-advantaged accounts are maxed, additional investing happens in a taxable brokerage. Use tax-efficient index funds (VTI, VOO, VXUS) and hold for years to benefit from long-term capital gains rates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>The Compound Growth Formula</h2>
    <div class="formula-box">
      FV = PMT × ((1 + r)<sup>n</sup> - 1) / r
    </div>
    <p>Where:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>FV</strong> = Future value</li>
      <li><strong>PMT</strong> = Periodic payment (monthly contribution)</li>
      <li><strong>r</strong> = Periodic rate (annual rate ÷ 12)</li>
      <li><strong>n</strong> = Total number of periods (years × 12)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Example:</strong> $1,000/month at 7% for 20 years. PMT = 1000, r = 0.00583, n = 240. FV ≈ $522,000. You contributed $240,000 — compound growth added $282,000.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AffiliateCTA partner={BETTERMENT} title="Stop overthinking investment allocation" blurb="Betterment automates portfolio rebalancing, tax-loss harvesting, and goal-based allocation. Five minutes to set up, then it runs itself while you focus on saving more." />

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What's a realistic return rate?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">7% real annual return is a reasonable baseline for a diversified equity portfolio over 20+ years. That's the long-term S&P 500 average after inflation. Plan around 7%, celebrate higher returns rather than depending on them.</div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Index funds vs individual stocks?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">Index funds win for 95% of investors. Lower fees (~0.05% vs 0.5-1.5%), better diversification, and they beat 85-90% of actively managed funds over 15+ year horizons. VTI + VXUS is a complete portfolio for most people.</div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Lump sum or DCA?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">Lump sum wins ~65% of the time historically. But DCA reduces regret risk if you'd lose sleep buying at a peak. Either is better than sitting in cash "waiting for a dip."</div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How much should I invest monthly?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">After 401(k) match and Roth IRA max, target 15-20% of gross income toward retirement. For shorter goals, work backward from the target — this calculator helps you find the required monthly contribution.</div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Should I keep investing during a market crash?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">Absolutely yes — that's when DCA does its best work. Continue your automated contributions through the dip. Trying to time when to re-enter is a losing game; investors who held through 2008-2009 saw their portfolios at all-time highs by 2014.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Related Calculators</h2>
    <div class="related-grid">
      <a href="/us/401k-calculator" class="related-card">
        <h3>401(k) Calculator</h3>
        <p>Tax-deferred retirement growth with employer match.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/us/roth-ira-calculator" class="related-card">
        <h3>Roth IRA Calculator</h3>
        <p>Tax-free retirement growth.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/us/compound-interest-calculator" class="related-card">
        <h3>Compound Interest Calculator</h3>
        <p>The fundamental compounding math.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
